BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 388.0 Td (  In the beginning, the world was a barren plain where the Weaver held five golden seeds. The Su) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 378.0 Td (    n, a generous deity, would triple whatever was cast into the earth, yet the earth itself rem) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 368.0 Td (    ained indifferent. The Weaver stood at the edge, hesitating. To hold the seeds was to surviv) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 358.0 Td (    e in meager safety; to cast them was to risk everything upon the honesty of another.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 348.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 338.0 Td (  The Weaver threw the seeds to the Gatherer, who stood in the center of the field. The Sun smil) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 328.0 Td (    ed, and the seeds bloomed into fifteen shimmering stalks. The Gatherer looked at the bounty,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 318.0 Td (     tempted to consume it all, yet remembered the Weaver's initial sacrifice. Understanding the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 308.0 Td (     sacred cycle, the Gatherer returned a bountiful portion to the Weaver, ensuring the next se) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 298.0 Td (    ason would dawn with promise.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 288.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 278.0 Td (  When trust reigned, the plain transformed into a lush forest, and both grew prosperous on the ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 268.0 Td (    surplus. But when the Gatherer grew selfish, hoarding the harvest, the Sun grew dim, and the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 258.0 Td (     fields withered into dust. The myth of Briar teaches that the golden seeds are not meant fo) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 248.0 Td (    r the palm, but for the cycle. To give is to sow; to return is to harvest. Without the rhyth) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 238.0 Td (    m of giving and receiving, even the richest field turns to stone.) Tj ET
BT /F2 8.0 Tf 48.0 228.0 Td (Reasoning:) Tj ET
