BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 468.0 Td (      In the age of the first loom, two weavers, Elara and Kael, sat on opposite banks of a silv) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 458.0 Td (        er river. Elara possessed the raw golden silk, while Kael held the enchanted loom that c) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (        ould weave threads into destiny. Alone, Elara's silk remained brittle, and Kael's loom s) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (        tood silent.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 428.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 418.0 Td (      The Great Pattern dictated that if Elara cast her silk across the water, the loom would we) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 408.0 Td (        ave it into robes of light, tripling its value. Yet, trust was a fragile bridge. If Kael) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 398.0 Td (         kept the light for himself, the river would drown them both in stagnation. If Elara hel) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 388.0 Td (        d back, the loom would never sing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 378.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 368.0 Td (      One solstice, Elara cast her entire store across the currents. Kael, moved by the faith be) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 358.0 Td (        stowed upon him, returned a share of the radiant cloth that exceeded what the silk alone) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 348.0 Td (         could have produced. Together, they draped the world in shimmering gold. The myth of th) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 338.0 Td (        e weaver's exchange teaches that prosperity is not a vessel to be guarded, but a harvest) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 328.0 Td (         to be shared. When one agent risks, and the other honors that risk with fairness, the f) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 318.0 Td (        abric of their world grows stronger, weaving a golden destiny that binds the two in eter) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 308.0 Td (        nal, prosperous resonance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 298.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 288.0 Td (      Here is the myth the other agent wrote in the previous round:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 278.0 Td (      In the age of the Great Wither, the valley of Aethelgard held only a single bushel of gold) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 268.0 Td (        en grain. Sol, the Keeper of the Seed, stood before the chasm that separated him from th) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 258.0 Td (        e fertile soil of the west. To cast the grain into the void was to risk everything, yet ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 248.0 Td (        he knew the earth would triple any gift entrusted to it.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 238.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 228.0 Td (      Luna, the Guardian of the Soil, waited on the far side. She held the power to nurture the ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 218.0 Td (        harvest or bury it in greed. If she kept the bounty for herself, the rains would cease, ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 208.0 Td (        and the valley would crumble into ash. If she returned a portion to Sol, the cycle of li) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 198.0 Td (        fe would flourish, and they would both feast in the coming seasons.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 188.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 178.0 Td (      Sol cast the seeds. They blossomed into a field of gold, tripled by the grace of the earth) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 168.0 Td (        . Luna looked upon the abundance. She saw that while she could hold it all, true prosper) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 158.0 Td (        ity was a bridge built on reciprocity. She offered back to Sol a fair share of the harve) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 148.0 Td (        st. Thus, the chasm was bridged, not by stone, but by the quiet understanding that to gi) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 138.0 Td (        ve is to receive, and to trust is to survive.) Tj ET
